      <h1><span class="chrome-text">How Close Can We Match Measurement and Simulation?</span></h1>
      <p class="post-subtitle">An X-band standard gain horn against NPL's 2023 measurement, within ±0.08 dB</p>
      <div class="post-byline"><span><strong>Dom Belgiovane, PhD</strong></span><span>November 2026</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="post container-narrow">
    <div class="measure">
      <p>In the last post, six solver runs of an X-band standard gain horn landed within ±0.0075 dB of each other. That proves the solvers agree. It does not prove they agree with the horn sitting on the range.</p>
      <p>That post ended with an error budget, and one line in it said "Physical versus model dimensions: unknown." This post is about that line, and about the comparison that matters most: simulation against a horn measured by a national standards laboratory.</p>
      <p>The work was published at the 2024 AMTA Symposium as "Antenna Gain Calibration with Improved Accuracy Modeling of Pyramidal Standard Gain Horns, Part 2," with Justin Dobbins and Afifeh Khatabi of Raytheon Technologies, and Andrea Giacomini, Francesco Saccardi and Lars J. Foged of Microwave Vision Italy.</p>

      <h2>Two horns that are supposed to be identical</h2>
      <p>We studied two commercial X-band NRL type horns: the original Scientific Atlanta (SA) Model 12-8.2 and the MVG SGH820. They were intended to have the same interior geometry. In reality they differ slightly. The SA horn has a 2.590 in waveguide section and a 0.125 in wall at the aperture. The SGH820 has a 1.331 in waveguide section and a 0.079 in wall. The aperture and flare length are within a thousandth of an inch of each other.</p>
      <p>The SA 12-8.2 was chosen for a reason. It is the horn from the international X-band horn gain intercomparison, so it has national laboratory measurements from NIST and NPL behind it, each with a published uncertainty.</p>
      <p>Both nominal designs were modeled in HFSS 2022 R2 with higher-order basis functions, electric and magnetic symmetry planes, a PML radiation boundary, and a λ/20 at 12 GHz mesh on the inner and outer aperture edges to capture edge diffraction. The two horns differ by less than 0.04 dB anywhere in the band.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="wide" style="max-width:760px">
      <div class="frame"><picture><source srcset="/blog/012-horn-sim-vs-measurement/images/fig04-sgh820-vs-sa-sim.webp" type="image/webp"><img src="/blog/012-horn-sim-vs-measurement/images/fig04-sgh820-vs-sa-sim.jpg" width="1200" height="759" alt="Simulated gain of the SA Model 12-8.2 and the MVG SGH820 from HFSS, with their difference on the right axis"></picture></div>
      <figcaption>Simulated gain of the two nominal horns. The difference stays between +0.027 dB and -0.039 dB. Source: Fig. 4, Belgiovane et al., AMTA 2024.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="measure">
      <h2>The edge of the horn has a history</h2>
      <p>Building the SA model meant some detective work. The drawings from SA, MI Technologies and NSI-MI are consistent across decades of production, but at least two publications used dimensions that are off-nominal, though still within manufacturing tolerance.</p>
      <p>We also found that the way the horns were made changed over the years. Serial numbers 1 and 2, likely built in the early 1970s, had the front edge beveled flat, perpendicular to the horn axis. Later horns skipped that finishing step and left a square-cut sheet metal edge. The SGH820 has both: its H-plane edges are flat and beveled from thick aluminum plate, and its E-plane edges are square-cut from thin sheet.</p>
      <p>That detail matters because the aperture edge is where the diffraction behind the boresight gain ripple comes from. Edge shape was not part of the tolerance sweep, but initial studies suggest it has a non-negligible effect on the peak-to-peak ripple.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="wide" style="max-width:620px">
      <div class="frame"><picture><source srcset="/blog/012-horn-sim-vs-measurement/images/fig03-aperture-edge-cut.webp" type="image/webp"><img src="/blog/012-horn-sim-vs-measurement/images/fig03-aperture-edge-cut.jpg" width="1049" height="421" alt="Square-cut and flat (beveled) aperture edge profiles of a standard gain horn"></picture></div>
      <figcaption>Square-cut versus flat (beveled) aperture edges. Source: Fig. 3, Belgiovane et al., AMTA 2024.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="measure">
      <h2>Which tolerance actually matters</h2>
      <p>We took the build tolerances from the manufacturing drawing and applied them to the SA 12-8.2 one at a time, including ±0.005 in on the waveguide width and height, ±0.040 in on the aperture width and height, ±0.060 in on the horn length, and ±0.010 in on the wall thickness. For each one we took the largest and smallest gain change from nominal across the band.</p>
      <p>One dimension dominates. The horn length moves the gain by +0.066 dB and -0.071 dB. Every other dimension stays around a hundredth of a dB or less, with wall thickness the largest of the rest at +0.010 dB and -0.012 dB.</p>
      <p>This was not a Monte Carlo study. Instead, every variable was set to its maximum, then to its minimum, and compared against the nominal model. Those corner cases moved the gain by +0.076 dB and -0.078 dB.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="wide" style="max-width:760px">
      <div class="frame"><picture><source srcset="/blog/012-horn-sim-vs-measurement/images/fig05-tolerance-bar-chart.webp" type="image/webp"><img src="/blog/012-horn-sim-vs-measurement/images/fig05-tolerance-bar-chart.jpg" width="1200" height="721" alt="Bar chart of the maximum and minimum gain change for each tolerance variable of the SA Model 12-8.2"></picture></div>
      <figcaption>Gain change from each tolerance variable alone, and from all of them together. Horn length dominates. Source: Fig. 5, Belgiovane et al., AMTA 2024.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="measure">
      <h2>The unknown gets a number</h2>
      <p>With the tolerance study done, the 2022 error budget could be updated:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Differences in code implementation: ±0.0075 dB</li>
        <li>Differences in physical versus model dimensions: ±0.0781 dB</li>
        <li>Higher-order waveguide modes, numerical precision, air box reflections, mesh noise: negligible</li>
        <li>Coax-to-waveguide adapter: measurable, still to be quantified</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The root sum square of those terms is ±0.0791 dB. Because it uses the extreme ends of the manufacturing tolerances and several commercial codes, it is a worst-case value, effectively a 100% confidence interval. It also assumes a perfectly symmetric horn, since the models used symmetry planes. A real horn may be slightly asymmetric, and that was not studied.</p>
      <p>The practical lesson is simple. Most of that uncertainty comes from not knowing the as-built dimensions, and a coordinate measuring machine can remove most of it. If you want to trust a simulated horn gain to a few hundredths of a dB, measure the horn, starting with its length.</p>

      <h2>The measurements</h2>
      <p>The intercomparison gives on-axis gain for SA 12-8.2 serial numbers 1 and 2. NIST measured them in 1992 from 8.2 to 12 GHz with a published uncertainty (2σ) of ±0.07 dB. NPL measured them in 1991 with a published uncertainty between ±0.04 and ±0.07 dB, depending on frequency. Both used the three-antenna extrapolation technique with the reference plane at the waveguide flange, so no adapter loss is included.</p>
      <p>Serial number 2 reads lower than serial number 1 above 10 GHz, and that was never explained in the original intercomparison. We used serial number 1 as the reference.</p>
      <p>In 2023, NPL measured the on-axis gain of an SGH820 with an uncertainty (2σ) of ±0.09 dB. The SGH820 has an integrated coax-to-waveguide adapter, so that measurement includes its loss. Even so, the two horn types agree fairly well.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="wide" style="max-width:760px">
      <div class="frame"><picture><source srcset="/blog/012-horn-sim-vs-measurement/images/fig08-npl-sa-vs-sgh820.webp" type="image/webp"><img src="/blog/012-horn-sim-vs-measurement/images/fig08-npl-sa-vs-sgh820.jpg" width="1200" height="761" alt="NPL gain measurements of SA Model 12-8.2 serial numbers 1 and 2 from 1991 and of an MVG SGH820 from 2023, with uncertainty bars"></picture></div>
