    </figure>

    <div class="measure">
      <h2>The legacy horn: close, with a bias</h2>
      <p>Against serial number 1, the nominal HFSS model is almost always higher than the measurement. Measured minus simulated ranges from +0.005 dB to -0.149 dB against NIST 1992, and from -0.027 dB to -0.166 dB against NPL 1991.</p>
      <p>That is respectable for a model built on nominal dimensions of a horn whose as-built dimensions are not available. It is also exactly the kind of gap the tolerance study says to expect when you do not know the horn length.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="wide" style="max-width:620px">
      <div class="frame"><picture><source srcset="/blog/012-horn-sim-vs-measurement/images/fig10-sa-sim-vs-nist-npl.webp" type="image/webp"><img src="/blog/012-horn-sim-vs-measurement/images/fig10-sa-sim-vs-nist-npl.jpg" width="1200" height="1628" alt="Measured IEEE gain of SA Model 12-8.2 serial number 1 from NIST 1992 and NPL 1991 compared with HFSS, with the difference on the right axis"></picture></div>
      <figcaption>SA 12-8.2 serial number 1: HFSS versus (a) NIST 1992 and (b) NPL 1991. Source: Fig. 10, Belgiovane et al., AMTA 2024.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="measure">
      <h2>The SGH820: within ±0.08 dB of NPL</h2>
      <p>For the SGH820 we built a high-fidelity model in CST using the FIT solver. It includes the coax-to-waveguide adapter section, the mixed flat and square-cut aperture edges, the machined exterior ribs, and the mounting plate at the back of the transition.</p>
      <p>One piece was left out: the short length of coaxial line that represents the field-replaceable SMA connector, which is a significant contributor to the antenna's ohmic loss. Rather than guess at it, we measured the insertion loss of the coax portion alone in a back-to-back configuration and subtracted it to get IEEE gain.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="wide" style="max-width:620px">
      <div class="frame"><picture><source srcset="/blog/012-horn-sim-vs-measurement/images/fig11-cst-highfidelity-model.webp" type="image/webp"><img src="/blog/012-horn-sim-vs-measurement/images/fig11-cst-highfidelity-model.jpg" width="1014" height="606" alt="High-fidelity CST model of the MVG SGH820 with exterior ribs, mounting plate and coax-to-waveguide adapter"></picture></div>
      <figcaption>High-fidelity CST model of the SGH820. Source: Fig. 11, Belgiovane et al., AMTA 2024.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="measure">
      <p>With the connector loss accounted for, simulation and the NPL 2023 measurement agree within ±0.08 dB, from +0.061 dB to -0.080 dB across the band. The two curves fall inside each other's uncertainty bounds, and the agreement is comparable to the published uncertainty of the calibration laboratory itself.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="wide" style="max-width:760px">
      <div class="frame"><picture><source srcset="/blog/012-horn-sim-vs-measurement/images/fig12-npl2023-vs-highfidelity.webp" type="image/webp"><img src="/blog/012-horn-sim-vs-measurement/images/fig12-npl2023-vs-highfidelity.jpg" width="1200" height="758" alt="NPL 2023 gain measurement of the MVG SGH820 compared with the high-fidelity CST simulation, with the difference on the right axis"></picture></div>
      <figcaption>NPL 2023 measurement of the SGH820 versus the high-fidelity CST FIT simulation. Source: Fig. 12, Belgiovane et al., AMTA 2024.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="measure">
      <h2>What about the NRL curve?</h2>
      <p>Most ranges still take SGH gain from the NRL curve, Slayton's 1954 geometric optics solution that leaves out aperture diffraction, and carry ±0.3 dB for it. We compared that curve with the CEM model of the nominal SA 12-8.2. The difference runs from +0.159 dB to -0.133 dB, so if the dimensions are known perfectly, the NRL curve is good to about ±0.16 dB, with no adapter.</p>
      <p>Slayton wrote that "in all probability, the actual errors are considerably less than the maximum possible tolerances quoted." He was right. Add the ±0.0791 dB for not knowing the as-built dimensions, and the maximum error from using the NRL curve comes to ±0.239 dB. That is remarkably close to his ±0.3 dB from seventy years ago.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="wide" style="max-width:760px">
      <div class="frame"><picture><source srcset="/blog/012-horn-sim-vs-measurement/images/fig09-cem-vs-nrl.webp" type="image/webp"><img src="/blog/012-horn-sim-vs-measurement/images/fig09-cem-vs-nrl.jpg" width="1200" height="819" alt="HFSS model of the SA Model 12-8.2 compared with the NRL gain curve, with the difference on the right axis"></picture></div>
      <figcaption>CEM model of the SA 12-8.2 versus the NRL gain curve. The ripple the NRL curve leaves out is the aperture diffraction. Source: Fig. 9, Belgiovane et al., AMTA 2024.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="measure">
      <h2>What this means on a range</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>±0.3 dB for the NRL curve is honest but conservative. With known dimensions it is closer to ±0.16 dB.</li>
        <li>A careful full-wave model of the horn you actually own can match a national laboratory measurement within that laboratory's own uncertainty.</li>
        <li>If you only measure one dimension of your reference horn, measure its length.</li>
        <li>The coax-to-waveguide adapter is the open term, and it still has to be quantified.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Paper (IEEE Xplore): <a href="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10789678" target="_blank" rel="noopener">https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/10789678</a></p>

      <h2>Future work</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Characterize the uncertainty of common coax-to-waveguide adapters, to finish the budget for the most common SGH configuration.</li>
        <li>Tighten the manufacturing tolerances on a high-fidelity standard gain horn so its on-axis gain uncertainty approaches the limits of the commercial EM solvers.</li>
        <li>Quantify the effects of aperture edge shape and horn asymmetry, which this work did not study.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Thanks to Raffaele Scuderi for his work on the CST simulations, and to the late Edwin "Ned" Barry, who was part of this paper early on and provided the history of the SA 12-8.2.</p>
    </div>

